**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 17 in full; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), All about electrochemistry completely; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.111SC, Fall 2014](../sources/MIT-5-111SC.lingua#r_312Z44VT67CCX60KG27PWM6NZ8), Unit IV’s redox and cell material; [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), electrochemical quantities and conventions.

An electrochemical cell couples reaction changes with charge transfer through an external path under specified conditions. Identify the electrodes, electrolyte, phases, half-reaction conventions, reaction extent, and electron amount. Oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode; electrode sign also depends on whether the cell is operated galvanically or electrolytically.

Tabulated standard electrode potentials use a reference and reduction convention. Combine them with a consistent balanced reaction, reversing the reaction account where necessary. Potential is an intensive quantity and is not multiplied by a stoichiometric factor as an energy would be. For electron-number coefficient z, the reversible reaction relation is Δ_rG = −zFE for the appropriately defined cell conditions.

Separate equilibrium/reversible cell potential from an operating terminal voltage with current, resistance, polarization, concentration gradients, and junction effects. A standard potential alone cannot predict the rate, power, stability, or actual performance of a device. Preserve the exact reference states and chemical species before comparing values.

**Work:** build complete reaction and energy accounts for textbook cell examples, checking charge and element balances. Explain reference-electrode dependence and why an individual half-cell value is not an independently measurable absolute electrical potential. Calculate the electron amount associated with a stated reaction extent.

Read the complete electrochemistry chapter and Chem1 unit, then the MIT redox/cell lectures. The later Nernst, electrolysis, and corrosion Records reuse this foundation, while batteries, power systems, and industrial electrochemistry will deepen their unique materials and operation.

**Continue:** [Use the Nernst relation with the actual reaction quotient](Nernst-quotients-and-cell-conditions.lingua#r_2V1NHR9TS7CD4ESB9PMAN3MJ5A).

